{%- comment -%}
  Two doors — the split directly under the hero film. Asked for 2026-09-13.

  WHAT THIS SECTION IS FOR, because it decides every choice below. The studio's
  own words: "tujuan website ini tuh utamanya bukan untuk jualan, tapi untuk
  biar wear label ini makin legit … intinya mau tekenin kalo ini tuh bisa untuk
  B2B". The site is a credibility surface first and a till second, and the thing
  it has never said loudly enough is that Wear Label produces custom apparel for
  organisations as well as trousers for shoppers. This band says both at once,
  in the first screenful after the film.

  IT IS TWO DOORS AND NOT A TAB STRIP, and that was argued out before it was
  built. A tab shows one side at a time, and the default tab would inevitably be
  ready-to-wear, because that is what most visitors arrive for — so a visitor who
  never thinks to click would never learn the second business exists. That is
  precisely the problem this band was commissioned to fix, moved somewhere else.
  Two panels side by side are read without being operated. They also need no
  script, have no state to keep in step, and survive a phone, where a tab strip
  would still be showing one side at a time.

  THE SPLIT IS 58/42 AND THE FIRST BLOCK GETS THE 58. That is not a claim that
  B2B is the bigger business — it is the smaller one. It is a correction for
  what the rest of the page already does: ready-to-wear has New arrivals, the
  voices wall and the Instagram strip below this band, three sections of proof,
  and custom apparel has one. The wider panel is what stops the first screenful
  from agreeing with the rest of the page instead of balancing it.

  So block order is load-bearing here in a way it usually is not. Reorder the
  blocks in the theme editor and the emphasis moves with them; there is no
  "which one is B2B" setting to get out of step with the order, on purpose.

  TONE IS A SETTING, NOT A POSITION, and the two available values are the two
  that can sit next to each other. Below `lg` the panels stack, and the repo's
  rule is that two bands of the same colour must not touch — so a build with
  both doors set to the same tone would read as one tall band with a seam in it.
  Espresso and cream is the pairing that carries the split down to a phone.
  `custom-band` documents the same trap from the other direction.

  THE PHOTOGRAPHS ARE SQUARE, both of them, and that is what lets two panels of
  different widths look deliberate rather than mismatched: the frames differ in
  size but not in shape. door-custom.webp is a crop of the studio's own hospital
  uniform shoot — six of the eight sets — and door-shop.webp is a square centre
  crop of hero-1.webp, the polaroid composition that led the old hero carousel
  and has had nothing pointing at it since the film replaced it.

  EACH PANEL IS ONE STRETCHED LINK, the pattern `product-card` uses: the anchor
  is on the heading and its `::after` covers the panel. A whole-panel `<a>` would
  have given the link an accessible name made of the eyebrow, the heading, the
  sentence and the button label read out as one run-on string. This way the
  pointer gets the whole panel and a screen reader gets the heading.

  The arrow slides on hover and the photograph scales a hair inside its own
  clipping frame. Both are transforms, so both are composited, which is the rule
  for any motion on this site.

  NO PRICE, NO PRODUCT COUNT AND NO CLIENT COUNT anywhere in here. The brief is
  explicit that the B2B side must not be inflated, and a number is the easiest
  way to inflate one.
{%- endcomment -%}

<section
  aria-labelledby="two-ways-heading"
  class="border-y border-rule"
>
  {%- comment -%}
    The band's own heading is not painted. The two panel headings are what a
    reader sees, and a third heading above them would be a label on a thing that
    already labels itself — but the section still needs a name in the
    accessibility tree, and `aria-labelledby` needs something real to point at.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <h2 id="two-ways-heading" class="sr-only">
    {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.heading, label: 'section heading', inline: true %}
  </h2>

  <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[58fr_42fr]">
    {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign tone = block.settings.tone

        if tone == 'invert'
          assign panel_bg = 'bg-invert'
          assign eyebrow_ink = 'text-ink-invert-muted'
          assign link_ink = 'text-ink-invert hover:text-ink-invert-muted'
          assign body_ink = 'text-ink-invert-muted'
          assign cta_ink = 'text-ink-invert'
        else
          assign panel_bg = 'bg-canvas'
          assign eyebrow_ink = 'text-ink-subtle'
          assign link_ink = 'text-ink hover:text-brand'
          assign body_ink = 'text-ink-muted'
          assign cta_ink = 'text-brand'
        endif

        assign door_href = block.settings.href | default: routes.all_products_collection_url
      -%}

      <div
        class="group relative flex flex-col p-7 sm:p-10 lg:p-12 {{ panel_bg }} {% unless forloop.last %}border-b border-rule lg:border-b-0 lg:border-r{% endunless %}"
        {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
      >
        {%- comment -%}
          THE CONTENT COLUMN IS CAPPED AND THE PANEL IS NOT. Without the cap the
          58% panel's square photograph is 835px on a 1440px screen against the
          42% panel's 605px, and the 230px of height that buys goes nowhere —
          it becomes a hole between the shorter panel's sentence and its link.
          Capping both columns at 640px makes the two photographs 640 and 605,
          which is near enough to level that the two links sit on the same line,
          while the panels themselves keep the widths that carry the emphasis.
          The difference a reader is supposed to see is the ground either side
          of the column, not the size of the picture.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="flex w-full max-w-160 flex-1 flex-col gap-6">
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-sm">
          {% render 'media-asset',
            image: block.settings.image,
            asset: block.settings.asset,
            alt: block.settings.alt,
            ratio: '1 / 1',
            asset_width: 1100,
            asset_height: 1100,
            sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 46vw, 92vw',
            label: 'Photo',
            class: 'transition-transform duration-(--duration-lead) ease-(--ease-entrance) motion-safe:group-hover:scale-[1.03]' %}
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
          <span class="text-micro uppercase tracking-eyebrow {{ eyebrow_ink }}">
            {% render 'copy', value: block.settings.eyebrow, label: 'eyebrow', inline: true %}
          </span>

          {%- comment -%}
            THE COLOUR IS ON THE ANCHOR, NOT ON THE HEADING, and that is not a
            tidy-up. base.css gives a link its own colour, which wins over a
            colour inherited from the h3 — so with the class on the heading the
            espresso panel drew its headline in brand taupe, rgb(114,94,76) on
            rgb(30,26,22). It rendered, it passed every check, and it was
            unreadable. footer.liquid and snippets/button.liquid both document
            the same trap; this is the third place it has bitten.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <h3 class="text-h2 leading-h2">
            <a
              href="{{ door_href }}"
              class="transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) after:absolute after:inset-0 after:content-[''] {{ link_ink }}"
            >{% render 'copy', value: block.settings.heading, label: 'heading', inline: true %}</a>
          </h3>

          <p class="wl-measure text-body leading-body {{ body_ink }}">
            {% render 'copy', value: block.settings.body, label: 'description', lines: 2 %}
          </p>
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          A span and not a button, and not a second anchor either: the panel is
          already one link, and an `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid markup that
          browsers repair by closing the outer one early. This is the affordance
          for the link that is already there.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if block.settings.cta != blank -%}
          <span class="mt-auto inline-flex items-center gap-2.5 text-label uppercase tracking-nav {{ cta_ink }}">
            {{ block.settings.cta }}
            <span class="inline-flex transition-transform duration-(--duration-base) ease-(--ease-entrance) motion-safe:group-hover:translate-x-1">
              {% render 'icon', icon: 'arrow-right', class: 'size-4' %}
            </span>
          </span>
        {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Two ways",
  "max_blocks": 2,
  "settings": [
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "The split under the hero. The FIRST door gets the wider column — that is how the emphasis is set, so reorder the blocks rather than looking for a setting. Give the two doors different tones: they stack on a phone, and two panels of the same colour touching read as one band." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Section heading", "default": "Two ways to work with Wear Label", "info": "Not painted — it names the section for screen readers only. The two panel headings are what a reader sees." }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "door",
      "name": "Door",
      "settings": [
        { "type": "select", "id": "tone", "label": "Tone", "default": "invert", "options": [
          { "value": "invert", "label": "Espresso" },
          { "value": "canvas", "label": "Cream" }
        ] },
        { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading" },
        { "type": "textarea", "id": "body", "label": "Description" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "cta", "label": "Link label" },
        { "type": "url", "id": "href", "label": "Link" },
        { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Photograph", "info": "Overrides the shipped asset below." },
        { "type": "text", "id": "asset", "label": "Shipped asset filename", "info": "A file in the theme's assets/. Square crops — the two panels differ in width and must not differ in shape." },
        { "type": "textarea", "id": "alt", "label": "Alt text", "info": "Used by the shipped asset only. An uploaded photograph carries its own alt from the Files admin." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Two ways",
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "door", "settings": { "tone": "invert" } },
        { "type": "door", "settings": { "tone": "canvas" } }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
